  "plan": {
    "plan_api": 2,
    "tier": "core",
    "count": 7,
    "aspects": [
      {
        "id": "adapt-first",
        "says": "A reference cannot adapt its argument, so a separate String::trim step runs before Integer::parseInt.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:8",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:9"
        ],
        "exercise": "clean-numbers",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "bifunction-chain",
        "says": "A two-argument unbound reference such as String::concat fits BiFunction, and its andThen chains a Function after it.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`BiFunction<T, U, R>` — `R apply(T t, U u)`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:5",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:31"
        ],
        "exercise": "slugs",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "bound-store",
        "says": "A bound reference such as processed::add is a Consumer that stores every value into that one list.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:1",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:23"
        ],
        "exercise": "audit-pipeline",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "collector-and-map-forms",
        "says": "Map.forEach takes a BiConsumer, and Collectors such as groupingBy take references as classifiers.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:18",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:19"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the page shows these as fragments; grouping by a reference is practised on 4.3.1 (top-earners) and collectors belong to the Streams module"
      },
      {
        "id": "comparing-keys",
        "says": "Comparator.comparing takes a key-extracting reference, and thenComparing adds tie-breakers for equal keys.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Comparator<T>` — `int compare(T o1, T o2)`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:6",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:25"
        ],
        "exercise": "multi-sort",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "consumer-chain",
        "says": "Consumer.andThen hands each value to the first consumer and then to the second.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Consumer<T>` — `void accept(T t)`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:14",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:23",
          "tests:15-method-references/src/test/java/com/github/msorkhpar/claudejavatutor/methodreferences/MethodReferencesWithFunctionalInterfacesTest.java"
        ],
        "exercise": "audit-pipeline",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "flatten-nested",
        "says": "Collection::stream in flatMap turns a list of lists into one stream.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:21"
        ],
        "exercise": "clean-numbers",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "function-order",
        "says": "f.andThen(g) runs f first and f.compose(g) runs g first, so the chosen method fixes the order of the steps.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Function<T, R>` — `R apply(T t)`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:3",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:29"
        ],
        "exercise": "slugs",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "generic-return",
        "says": "A reference to a generic method, such as Collections::emptyList, takes its type argument from the target.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:20"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "type inference at compile time, with nothing a test can observe once it compiles"
      },
      {
        "id": "interface-match",
        "says": "Which functional interface a reference fits depends on the number and types of its arguments and on its return type.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Key Points to Remember"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "the thread through every practice on this page, each of which fixes the interface its references must fit"
      },
      {
        "id": "named-comparators",
        "says": "Named Comparator constants built from references keep sort rules in the domain class.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:16"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "a way of declaring a comparator, not a behaviour; multi-sort builds the same kind of chain"
      },
      {
        "id": "negation",
        "says": "The ! operator cannot negate a method reference; Predicate.not can.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:10",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:11"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "no-own-type",
        "says": "In comparing(...).reversed() a lambda's parameter gets no target type and is inferred as Object, while a method reference still compiles.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:15"
        ],
        "exercise": "quiz",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "optional-flatmap",
        "says": "A reference that returns an Optional belongs in flatMap, or the result nests as Optional<Optional<T>>.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Edge Cases and Their Handling",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:17"
        ],
        "exercise": "optional-lookup",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "optional-map-filter",
        "says": "Optional's map and filter take references and pass an empty Optional through untouched.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Optional<T>` — `map`, `filter`, `ifPresent`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:7"
        ],
        "exercise": "optional-lookup",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "predicate-combine",
        "says": "Predicates built from static and unbound references combine with and, or and Predicate.not.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Predicate<T>` — `boolean test(T t)`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:4",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:27"
        ],
        "exercise": "named-predicates",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "reversed-scope",
        "says": "reversed() reverses the whole comparator it is called on, so one key is reversed by reversing its own comparator.",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:25"
        ],
        "exercise": "multi-sort",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "supplier-lazy",
        "says": "A Supplier defers its work until get() is called, so a factory handed to orElseGet runs only when the value is missing.",
        "basis": [
          "section:`Supplier<T>` — `T get()`",
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:2"
        ],
        "exercise": "optional-lookup",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "typed-array",
        "says": "toArray(String[]::new) collects a stream into a String[] rather than an Object[].",
        "basis": [
          "example:15-method-references/README_4.3.3.md:35"
        ],
        "exercise": "named-predicates",
        "reason": null
      },
      {
        "id": "version-notes",
        "says": "Java 9 to 21 add methods such as Optional.ifPresentOrElse, Predicate.not and Stream.toList that take or pair with references.",
        "basis": [
          "section:Relevant Java 21 Features"
        ],
        "exercise": null,
        "reason": "version notes; Predicate.not and toList are used throughout this page's practices"
      }
    ],
